INT. SHUTTERED BOWLING ALLEY - IMPROVISED ARBITRATION HALL - DAY

Lanes polished to a dull glow. A folding table placed at the foul line, three chairs, a pitcher of water. Behind them, the pin deck, bare. Dust floats in light from the high windows.

ARBITRATOR ODETTE MARCHAND, 60s, in a charcoal suit, sits at the head with a fountain pen and a legal pad. She hasn't blinked in a minute.

COUNSEL ELLIOT VESPER, 40s, sleeves rolled, stands at the lane's edge with a stack of exhibits. His client sits across from him: KHALID RAHMAN, 50s, a contractor's work boots and a clean white shirt, hands folded.

VESPER
Mr. Rahman, you signed the original contract in March.

RAHMAN
Yes.

VESPER
And you understood that it covered the demolition and remediation of the east wing.

RAHMAN
I understood that it covered what I was told it covered.

VESPER
Told by whom?

RAHMAN
By you.

Marchand's pen scratches. Vesper smiles thinly.

VESPER
Mr. Rahman, we have a signature on page nineteen.

RAHMAN
You have a signature on page nineteen. You do not have a signature on page twenty. Because page twenty does not exist.

Vesper's smile stays. His shoulders tighten.

VESPER
Madam Arbitrator, the witness is characterizing.

MARCHAND
Overruled. Continue.

VESPER
Mr. Rahman. Is it your testimony that the contract was never finalized?

RAHMAN
It's my testimony that the contract you handed me was a draft, and the one you filed with this panel is a forgery.

The word drops into the lane like a ball. Nothing rolls.

MARCHAND
(quietly)
Counsel, that is a serious accusation.

VESPER ^
Madam Arbitrator, I must --

RAHMAN
Seven years I have built hospitals. Seven. I have never once been accused of cutting corners. And you come here with a paper that says I did.

VESPER
Sit down, Mr. Rahman.

RAHMAN
I am sitting.

VESPER
Then be quiet.

RAHMAN
Or what?

Vesper leans over the table, close. Marchand puts down her pen with a click that rings off the pins.

MARCHAND
Counsel. Step back.

He steps back. Slow. He adjusts his cuffs, a tic.

MARCHAND (CONT'D)
Mr. Vesper. How long have you been practicing?

VESPER
Eighteen years.

MARCHAND
And in those eighteen years, have you ever once been handed a document that you knew to be incomplete?

VESPER
Never, Madam.

MARCHAND
Then why does the metadata on exhibit twelve show it was created on Tuesday?

Vesper does not answer. Rahman looks at him, then at the arbitrator, then at his own hands.

RAHMAN
I did not want to say.

MARCHAND
Say it.

RAHMAN
I do not care about the money. I care that he is not ashamed.

Marchand folds her hands. The high windows glow. A single bowling ball, stranded on the return rack, catches the light.

MARCHAND
Mr. Vesper. I am suspending this hearing. I am referring exhibit twelve to the bar.

VESPER
(barely)
Yes, Madam.

He gathers his papers. They shake. The sound of paper is the loudest thing in the building.
